artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ be a bounded smooth domain, let $0 < \alpha < 1$, let $1 < p < (N+2)/(N-2)$ (with $p$ arbitrary if $N \le 2$), and let $\lambda > 0$. Consider the singular semilinear elliptic problem
$$
-\Delta u + u^{-\alpha}\chi_{\{u>0\}} = \lambda u^{p}, \quad u \ge 0 \text{ in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \text{ on } \partial\Omega. \quad (\mathrm{II})
$$
For $\varepsilon > 0$, regularize the singular absorption $u^{-\alpha}\chi_{\{u>0\}}$ by a smooth approximation (e.g. replacing $u^{-\alpha}\chi_{\{u>0\}}$ by $(u+\varepsilon)^{-\alpha}$ or a similar truncation) so that the associated energy functional $J_\varepsilon$ on $H_0^1(\Omega)$ is well defined and of mountain-pass geometry. Assume that for each $\varepsilon > 0$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $J_\varepsilon$ attains a nonnegative local minimum $u_\varepsilon \ge 0$;
\item $J_\varepsilon$ has a second nonnegative critical point $v_\varepsilon \ge 0$ obtained via the Mountain Pass Theorem;
\item as $\varepsilon \to 0^+$, $u_\varepsilon \to u$ and $v_\varepsilon \to v$ in a suitable sense, where $u, v \ge 0$ are two distinct nonnegative weak solutions of (II).
\end{itemize}

Question: Must the limit mountain-pass solution $v$ necessarily have compact support in $\Omega$ (equivalently, the set $\{v = 0\}$ has nonempty interior in $\Omega$), as opposed to being strictly positive throughout $\Omega$?

